1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
December two thousand and eight. The committee that dates

2
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:07,203
American recessions announces that one has begun. It began

3
00:00:07,303 --> 00:00:10,379
a year earlier. All year the quarterly numbers had

4
00:00:10,479 --> 00:00:13,300
argued: a bad quarter, a good one, then worse.

5
00:00:13,566 --> 00:00:16,050
Nobody tells us the state of the economy while

6
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:17,959
we are in it. We have to infer it.

7
00:00:19,033 --> 00:00:21,595
The state of the economy is hidden weather. Today

8
00:00:21,695 --> 00:00:23,713
we learn to read it from a thermometer.

9
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:28,820
Picture a machine behind a curtain, with two states: expansion

10
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:33,073
and recession. Each quarter it stays or switches. From expansion

11
00:00:33,173 --> 00:00:36,927
it stays with probability p, about nought point nine; from

12
00:00:37,027 --> 00:00:40,582
recession with probability q, about nought point seven.

13
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:42,695
So an expansion lasts about ten quarters

14
00:00:42,795 --> 00:00:44,824
on average, and a recession about three.

15
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:49,420
Each state has its own distribution for GDP growth: centred near

16
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:53,323
one percent a quarter in expansion, slightly below zero in recession.

17
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:56,698
Let it run. Each quarter the machine hops, then draws

18
00:00:56,798 --> 00:00:59,647
one number from the bell of its current state.

19
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:06,324
Now draw the curtain. The colours vanish, and this grey series is

20
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:10,224
all we ever see. How far should each number move our belief?

21
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,238
Hold a belief: a needle between the two states. Before the next

22
00:01:15,338 --> 00:01:20,110
quarter, the needle relaxes toward the long-run mix, because the weather can

23
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:24,470
change. That is the predict step: multiply by the transition matrix.

24
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:27,654
Then a number arrives, and we read the height of each bell

25
00:01:27,754 --> 00:01:30,951
at that point. Growth of one point two percent is four times

26
00:01:31,051 --> 00:01:34,523
more likely under expansion, so the needle swings toward the sun.

27
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:39,882
Minus one percent is the opposite case: there the recession bell

28
00:01:39,982 --> 00:01:43,999
is far taller. Multiply each height by its prior weight, divide

29
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:47,659
by the total, and the needle swings past the middle. One

30
00:01:47,759 --> 00:01:51,776
bad quarter is strong evidence, not proof. This is Bayes' rule,

31
00:01:51,876 --> 00:01:54,586
and it is the whole of the Hamilton filter.

32
00:01:55,666 --> 00:02:00,313
Now the real thing: quarterly US GDP growth from nineteen fifty-two

33
00:02:00,413 --> 00:02:06,122
to nineteen eighty-four, Hamilton's sample, with the official recessions shaded in

34
00:02:06,222 --> 00:02:10,090
grey. We fit the model by maximum likelihood and run the

35
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:14,270
filter forward, one quarter at a time, never looking ahead.

36
00:02:14,366 --> 00:02:17,421
Watch the red line. It finds every recession in the

37
00:02:17,521 --> 00:02:20,886
sample, usually within a quarter, from GDP alone. It was

38
00:02:20,986 --> 00:02:24,846
never told the committee's dates; it only reads the thermometer.

39
00:02:24,933 --> 00:02:29,412
After nineteen eighty-four the economy grew calmer, and the model then needs

40
00:02:29,512 --> 00:02:33,088
a separate variance for each state. The idea does not change.

41
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:37,815
So: the regime is hidden, but every observation is evidence.

42
00:02:37,915 --> 00:02:41,814
Predict with the transition matrix, update with Bayes' rule, and

43
00:02:41,914 --> 00:02:45,500
the filtered probability tells us, in real time, how likely

44
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,937
it is that we are already in the storm.

